<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!-- How far the drawing sits from the window's bottom-right corner. The default clears the 8 DIP
         transparent margin that most of these windows leave around their visible card by 2 DIP, which is
         what puts the lines just inside the card; a window whose corner is further in overrides this in its
         own resources (see QuickLookWindow and the two LocalSend windows). A DynamicResource rather than a
         StaticResource, so that per-window override wins: a template's own dictionary would resolve a
         static lookup to this value before the window ever got a say. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="ResizeGripInset">4,4,10,10</Thickness>

    <!-- The resize grip WPF draws for ResizeMode="CanResizeWithGrip", restyled.

         It is not a control of ours: Window creates a System.Windows.Controls.ResizeGrip (a Control, not
         a Thumb) and sizes it against the bottom-right corner whenever ResizeMode is CanResizeWithGrip,
         and that type takes its look from a theme style keyed by its own type. An implicit style here
         therefore replaces the theme's without any of that machinery being touched, which matters because
         the grip is also the only thing that hit-tests for CanResizeWithGrip (Window.HandleWmNcHitTestMsg
         checks nothing else), so a window that dropped the type would stop resizing altogether.

         Three stepped lines rather than the theme's dotted triangle: the theme's grip is a small field of
         dots that reads as decoration at this size, and the corner rule Windows itself draws is
         unmistakable and gives the eye something to aim at. Drawn faint, the same as the window chrome it
         sits against.

         ALIGNMENT IS THE POINT. The element Window creates spans the window's bottom-right corner, window
         rect included, so a template rooted in a plain Grid puts its drawing wherever that Grid's own
         alignment puts it. The root here is aligned bottom-right and inset by ResizeGripInset, which lands
         the lines just inside the visible card rather than in the transparent margin outside it. That
         margin is where a resize band would need WindowChrome to work, and it does not for a transparent
         window, so a grip drawn out there is one the pointer cannot reach.

         KEYLESS, AND IT HAS TO LIVE HERE rather than in a window's own dictionary. Resources/Styles.xaml
         is merged once by App.xaml, so this style is in Application.Resources and every window that asks
         Windows for a grip picks it up. That is what the grip needs, because seven windows set
         ResizeMode="CanResizeWithGrip" (search, quick panel, quick look, settings, both LocalSend windows,
         the plugin field prompt) and the grip is not a control any of them declares: a window can only
         inherit it, never restyle it by declaring something, so a per-window dictionary reaches just the
         windows that remember to merge that dictionary. -->
    <Style TargetType="{x:Type ResizeGrip}">
        <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Right"/>
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Bottom"/>
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="{x:Type ResizeGrip}">
                    <Path Stroke="{DynamicResource WindowChromeText}" StrokeThickness="1" Opacity="0.5"
                          Margin="{DynamicResource ResizeGripInset}"
                          Data="M 12,0 L 0,12 M 12,4 L 4,12 M 12,8 L 8,12"/>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
